I started out attempting to use my pet dog as a shield to descend into
the mines, hope for Minetown instead of Orctown, and buy some
protection. It was going well, until I stepped on a teleportation trap
and found myself in the midst of a snake, a hobbit and a couple of
angry gnomes, They pelted me with daggers and bolts, but, thankfully,
I was able to blind them with my camera and avoid death until I could
get back to my dog. But then Gus stepped on a level teleporter and any
hope of making it down to the temple to buy protection was gone.

Luckily, I'd donned some of the armor from my dog's meals and had
found a pair of Gauntlets of Power and a pair of jumping boots on the
dungeon floor. So with an AC of 3, I was buffed up enough to fend for
myself, aside from the fact that the Dwarven Mattock I wielded in
desperation turned out to be cursed. Nothing a bit of prayer couldn't
fix, though, so in no time, I was level 5.

As it turned out, Minetown was Grotto Town and not overrun by a
maurauding orc band. In fact, it turned out to totally be my kind of
town. No so much, though, for a knight named Braylor. Judging by the
crime scene, he'd angered the watch and fell afoul of their rather
harsh form of frontier justice. His loss was my gain. I donned his
cursed elven mithril and absconded with his cursed magic whistle and
uncursed saddle. Poking around a bit more, I found that Izchak carried
nothing but candles, which was disappointing. But the temple was co-
aligned and in the tool shop I discovered a magic harp! I paid full
price, which was only 67 zorkmids. Besides, I figured that once I had
a powerful enough pet, I'd get my money back and then some.

The local grocer had some bottled water on sale, so after a small
purchase and a trip to the temple, I had holy water in hand. Also, I
killed a black unicorn and got a unicorn horn, which allowed me to
ditch the cumbersome mattock. Finally, after exhausting a bag of
tricks I=92d found on the dungeon floor, The Lady granted me a gift: +5
blessed Bronze Plate Mail! Heavy, but with my gauntlets of power, it
was doable and offered much better protection than my now uncursed
elven mithril.

Next stop, Sokoban, complete with a new pet dog. En route to the
puzzle tower, I took a detour through an armor shop on level 5 that
was selling speed boots. Score. Also picked up another stray pooch and
a pony before puzzling.

Sokoban was where I made my first big mistake. On the top level, I got
hit by a werewolf who, despite my dwarven cloak, infected me with
lycanthropy. I didn't notice, however, until I turned into a wolf and
destroyed both my cloak and my armor. Hard to miss that. No way was I
going to face the zoo with no MC or body armor. So I dove back down to
the mine temple to retrieve another cloak and the mithril I=92d so
recently discarded before returning. Clearing the zoo was a piece of
cake and, with the amulet of reflection, it was time to find the
luckstone.

Now that I was better protected, I fully explored the two mine levels
above Grotto Town and discovered a magic lamp! What=92s more, a trip to
the temple showed it was already blessed! A little spit and polish
summoned a rightfully grateful Djinni, who rewarded me with =93blessed
+2 very burnt gray dragon scale mail.=94 Sigh =96 always say =93fixed.=94
Always, always, always.

Also, I made a wise choice after BUC testing a bunch of rings I=92d
brought back from Sokoban. I was just about to try them on in the
temple when I said, =93Hmmm. If one of these is conflict, that priestess
may very well kick my ass.=94 And, in fact, once I got back out into the
open, one of the watchmen wielded his short sword the instant I put
one of my rings on. Conflict? Check.

The rest of the mine was fairly uneventful. I had to chomp into a
lizard corpse at one point thanks to a cockatrice. As for my brood of
pets, one suffered a grisly death at the hands of =85 er =85. something I
didn=92t see, but a little polymorph trap hopscotch transformed Gus into
a herzou and Bessie the pony into a chickatrice. I left Bessie to fend
for herself.

I ran into a hairy situation about halfway down. A dwarf read a scroll
of create monster, and suddenly I was facing a horde of monsters,
including some tough soldiers, in an open area. I high tailed it back
to a nearby corridor (thank you speed boots), teleported my pets away
and slipped on the ring of conflict. Crisis averted.

Finding the luckstone was a bit of a pain, as it was the Mimic of the
Mines and some thieving Dwarf had found the treasure before me in the
one unguarded nook where it lay. Took a while to figure out what had
happened, but locate the stone I did, but only after having to waste a
vial of holy water on a loadstone. Now it was time to use my demon
friend to take care of the shopkeepers and buy some protection.

The plan went swimmingly (I left Izchak alone. of course). I bought
four or five points of protection and then spent some quality time
with the altar and a couple of wands of create monster. And got a
gift! A blessed fireproof sling named Giantslayer! Wheeee!

Then, my second mistake. The wand summoned a gas spore and, without
thinking, I attacked it with my unicorn horn. It exploded and, boy,
did that piss off the priestess of the Lady. I didn=92t want to lose my
points of protection by killing the priestess, but I also wanted to
keep the altar. Thanks to the two scrolls of Earth from Sokoban, I was
able to do both by trapping her on the side of the temple and chopping
through the boulders to access the altar safely. I=92d have to wait to
buy more protection, but all in all, I think I came out of that minor
crisis fairly well.

Delving deeper into the dungeon revealed more bounty for my lucky
tourist =96 an altar (quickly converted to the Lady) and a cursed magic
lamp. After blessing it I decided I=92d wait to see what I needed before
giving it a rub. For the moment, I was very well outfitted with great
armor, speed boots a moderately enchanted unicorn horn. Meanwhile, Gus
the Herzou found himself pining for the home fires of hell and
therefore threw himself mercilessly at a blue jelly until he expired.
Sad. Soon after, I fell through a trapdoor and into the Big Room.

I killed off a few of the room=92s denizens before they threatened to
swarm me and then dug down with a wand of digging. After I found the
upstair, the big room posed very little trouble and had the added
bonus of the magic portal to the Quest. But I wasn=92t quite ready.

Descending deeper, I found another temple =96 Blind Io=92s =96 and a potion
shop, but nothing much else of note. Thanks to another polymorph trap,
I rode an Orange Dragon around for a while, which later ended up on
the losing end of a fight with an Ochre Jelly. When I emerged on
Medusa=92s level, it was mostly water and ravens, and without some means
of levitation or water walking, I decided it was time to take on the
Quest. And since I knew the Quest would generate artifact No. 2, I
figured no time was better than the present to go for Grayswandir. The
fates smiled on me. Grayswandir was finally mine, a saber that I=92d
only seen once before. I was playing a monk when my god granted it to
me and it was, of course, completely useless, as I was playing
Sporkhack. Luckily, back then, I had a Titan for a pet, who used it to
great advantage. Unfortunately, while he was taking a lunch break, a
rock troll pounded that poor character into a frothy pudding around
dungeon level 14. I=92ve always wondered whether some poor unfortunate
soul ran into that Grayswandir-wielding Titan in a bones level.

Of course, as I always do on the Tourist quest, I took a dip in the
pool, thinking it was a fountain. Luckily, I survived even though I
was burdened (I thought that was instadeath?) and got away with just a
couple of scrolls blanked. I promptly greased my bag, regreased
everything else and carried on.

Identify scrolls were plentiful, but the shops didn=92t have what I=92d
hoped for: a bag of holding. By the time I got to the final Quest
level, I was so burdened I had to create a temporary stash and return
to my main stash on level 12 to drop off some junk. Upon my return, I
fought the Master of Thieves who has clearly been buffed up quite a
bit from vanilla. He hit very hard and I had a tough time taking him
down, even though I was dual weaponing with Grayswandir and an elvish
short sword. But take him down I did and the Bell and fabled charge
card were mine.

With the bounty of ID scrolls I=92d looted from the Quest, I was finally
able to identify my entire stash, which revealed I had a cursed ring
of levitation and a blessed scroll of genocide. Typically, I don=92t use
these things but the opportunity was just too tempting. Gave =93L=94 a go
and, luckily, arch-liches and master liches were genocided. What a
difference it made!

Medusa=92s level was a cinch, followed by two tastes of Gehennom, a
normal dungeon level and the Castle which, again, posed no problems.
In fact, the armory had a cloak of magic resistance and the wand was
0:3. Amazing.

I wished up a silver short sword (Tourists only get up to skilled in
two-weaponing and at that skill level, the biggest weapon I can handle
in my off-hand was a short sword =96 to compensate, the game allows
silver daggers and short swords), a bag of holding and a magic marker,
buffed up my weapons and armor, swapped out my GDSM for some gold
dragon scale mail (permanent light source), got myself crowned (hello
Vorpy!) and descended into Gehennom riding a Jabberwock named =93Jib-
Jab!=94 that I=92d tamed with my harp.

Gehennom was thankfully fairly straightforward. Dual weaponing with a
fully enchanted Grayswandir and silver short sword cut through most
enemies like a power saw through gelatin and my blessed +6 poisoned
darts took out the few mind flayers that I encountered from distance.
The only demon lord that really put up a fight was Asmodeus, and even
with him I didn=92t need to resort to a potion of full healing.  I
polymorphed into a Vampire Lord before taking on Vlad, so Lifestealer
didn=92t cause me any serious harm.

What=92s more, I found yet another blessed magic lamp deep in Gehennom.
Now armed with a permanent light source, I swapped out my gold dragon
scale mail for blue dragon scale mail (speed) and changed back into my
old pair of jumping boots.

Jib-Jab! was a lot of fun, but shortly before I undertook the final
ascension run, a Magic Eye slapped him with =93Slow=94 and, unlike me, he
just couldn=92t shake it. I=92d just have to get to the planes on my own.
I wished up a second wand of death and took the plunge.

Rodney went down in a single blast from Orcus=92 wand and the race was
on. I performed the invocation, decended down, and then quickly came
back up to teleport back down with a cursed scroll of teleportation.
Luckily (this was a trend in this game), I appeared next to the temple
of Moloch. Finding the secret door was a bit tricky, as my cloak of
magic resistance was not fireproofed =96 a spellcaster had stripped off
the fireproofing with destroy armor and, though I=92d brought it back up
to snuff with the repair armor spell, I=92d not bothered to reproof it.
It only got =93burned=94 though before the secret door was revealed and
the priest of Moloch went down on the second blast from the wand of
death. Grabbed the amulet, quaffed a cursed potion of gain level, and
the race was on.

Ascending to the planes was blessedly easy. Rodney appeared twice and
double troubled me once, but the WoD took him down before he could
cause any serious mischief. The only serious trouble appeared when I
entered the plane of fire. An Archon was standing in between me and
the portal and, stupidly, I had no way to blind myself.

You see, in the latest version of Sporkhack, telepathy grows gradually
with every shaman or floating eye you eat. In the beginning, it=92s a
very weak power, with a range of just a few squares, but by the end of
the game, if you munch wisely, it grows into a power that covers most
of the map even when you=92re not blinded. Once I ditched my camera to
save on weight, I didn=92t think to acquire another means of blinding
myself.

In retrospect, I should have burned a wish for a blessed blindfold.
Or, at least, I should have dropped a scroll of scare monster when
things got hairy. Instead, I tried to race the Archon. Bad move. He
stunned and blinded me, then summoned nasties and cast =93Berzerk=94 which
gives all monsters in his vision a seriously scary to-hit and damage
bonus. I quaffed 7 of my 10 potions of full healing, broke a wand of
teleportation to clear the area and, finally, recovered from stunning
while remaining blinded long enough to kill the Archon. I suppose I
could have picked up his Sunsword for more points, but given what I=92d
just been through, I decided it would be better to make a beeline to
the portal.

With just 3 potions of full healing, the Astral Plane was scary. I
plopped down in the middle of 4 angels of Moloch, a demilich and a red
dragon, who pounded me with magic missiles (in Spork, magic resistance
and reflection are only a partial protection against magic missiles)
and big time body blows. One potion gone before my minion and I could
kill or teleport these baddies away. Once the coast was relatively
clear, I summoned up my army of tame purple worms (cursed scroll of
genocide + my magic harp) and headed to the middle altar, guarded by
Death.

Death got in a couple of good blows before succumbing, and my worms
did a nice job of gobbling up priests and heroes. Speed plus jumping
boots is a great combination, and I was very thankful for the rings of
Free Action and Regeneration, which I know saved my life more than
once here. The middle altar was chaotic, so I trudged back to the
lefthand altar, guarded by Famine, taken out by a wand of death. This
altar, alas, was Blind Io=92s. My army was dwindling, but, thankfully, I
had one last scroll of genocide and some unholy water. My second army
and I trudged across the plane to face Pestilance, who sickened me
several times, consuming the last of my full healing potions,
including a couple I=92d found on the ground and the two that I burned
my last wish for. The wand of death finally found its mark, though,
and with my wand of teleportation, I cleared a path to the altar,
where my guardian angel and I ascended to our reward. The last two
surviving purple worms were too busy eating to bother.

Typical.

